\chapter{ExoML contracts and target-specific CDecls}
\label{ch:95}
\index{ExoML contracts and target-specific CDecls}
\index{FFI!semantic contracts}

A C declaration describes ABI structure. The ExoML semantic sidecar can additionally describe validity conditions, ownership, transfer, borrows, failure conventions and effects. Target-qualified extraction pins the source and toolchain evidence behind the declaration. Verification compares recorded metadata and artifacts with their source provenance. Keep the full contract when a coarse compatibility projection drops conditions such as transfer on success only.

\section{Trace a two-outcome ownership contract}
Consider a native call whose argument is retained on success but remains with the caller on failure. A type-correct C declaration alone does not specify which branch owns the argument. Record the two outcomes explicitly and test the exact provider behavior in both states. This documentation is necessary for designing adapters, and general conditional foreign ownership still requires appropriate compiler support. \OnxCodeTerm{on1x-bind} can inspect and validate supported contracts and emit bounded adapters where eligible. Read the review requirements before asking for a safe declaration.

\section{Follow the three lifetimes in a JSON array}
The C contract example creates an array root and a numeric child
containing 42. Before insertion there are two independent obligations to
destroy objects. If either allocation fails, the example takes its
cleanup branch; if append fails, it takes the same branch because
ownership has not been transferred. Count the obligations again after
a successful append: there is now one root to destroy. Clearing the
local child pointer documents the transfer but does not itself destroy
anything. A binding that unconditionally released the child after the
call would be wrong precisely on this successful branch.

Next, indexing the root at zero returns a borrowed child. Reading its
number while the root is alive gives 42. Stringifying the root creates
another object with different rules: the serialized text has an
independent allocation. After destroying the root, printing that
successful serialization and freeing it is legitimate. Reading the
indexed child is no longer legitimate. Both values came from queries
on the same root; treating every query result as ``valid until root
destruction'' would misclassify the text as well.

As an annotation exercise, underline the facts a declaration extractor
can recover: parameter and result types and their target layout. Circle
the facts that require behavioral evidence: which result code commits
the insertion, which query aliases the tree, and which deallocator
releases the serialization. A complete declaration inventory can agree
with every underlined fact while leaving every circled question open.

One quick review question is whether destroying the root on the error
branch also destroys the proposed child. Only successful insertion
connects the two objects, so on this branch two cleanups are necessary
when both allocations succeeded.

